course: dictionary
lesson: 8659
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. Seat can be a noun, which is a thing, and it can be a verb, which is an action. The words around it tell you which meaning is right. «Please take a seat by the window.» Here seat is a thing you sit on.
explain: «The table seats six people.» Here seats is a verb, and it means has room for. Ask yourself: is this word a thing or an action? Then try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Seat can also mean a place in a theatre or on a plane, or a place won in an election. «She won a seat on the council.» Context shows you which meaning the writer means.
fact: A noun names a thing, and a verb names an action.
fact: As a noun, «seat» means something to sit on, like a chair, a bench or a place on a bus.
fact: As a verb, «seat» means to give someone a place to sit, or to have room for people.
fact: «Please be seated» is a polite way to say «sit down».
fact: «Seat» can also mean a place in a parliament or council won in an election.
exercise: «A seat is for a person, so put your bag on your lap or the floor when the bus is busy.» What is a seat in this sentence? | *A place for one person to sit | The floor of the bus | A bag for your shopping | A rule about buses
exercise: «At the table you wait until everyone is seated and served.» What does «seated» mean here? | *Given a place to sit | Moved to the front | Fed and washed | Hidden under the table
exercise: «The table seats six.» How many people can the table seat? | Four | *Six | Sixty | Two
test: «Please be seated.» What does this polite phrase mean? | *Sit down | Close your eyes | Stand up and sing | Go away
test: «She was seated by the window.» Is seated a verb or a noun here, and what does it mean? | *A verb meaning she was given a place to sit | A verb meaning she ran away | A noun meaning a chair | A noun meaning a bus stop
exam: Which is the best way to find the meaning of seat in an unknown sentence? | *Read the words around it and ask which meaning fits | Guess the first meaning you think of | Skip the word and never read the sentence again | Count the letters and pick the longest meaning
